Abstract
One of the key challenges in rational design and synthesis of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) is defined control over size and morphology for using these materials in many more advanced applications. Combining ultrasonic method and capping groups makes it possible to control the size and change the morphology of metal organic frameworks without changing material compositions. Nano TMU-46, 47 and 48 with copper metal center and amide functionalized pillar were synthesized by using ultrasonic irritation and characterized by FE-SEM, powder X-ray diffraction, elemental analysis, and FTIR spectroscopy. Sensing properties of these nano scale materials and the correlation between the nano structure and nitroaromatic compounds detection were studied. Nano TMU-46 due to its special and porous structure and available functional group show strong luminescence emission, which is selectively sensitive to Trinitrophenol (TNP) with a detection limit of $10^{-3}\mathbf{M}$ . Furthermore, multiple morphological transition in the structure of these crystals were observed with using different modulators. This morphology transition, in fact, successfully led to significant change in sensing properties of this structure and decreased the detection limit to $10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ .
Keywords: Metal-organic Framework; Nano cubic; Ultrasound; Luminescence; Trinitrophenol (TNP);
## Introduction
Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) can be described as a new class of porous hybrid materials which are formed from metal ions and organic linkers.[1-3] One of the most interesting properties of these crystalline framework over the other porous materials is ability to tune their size and functionality to extend their capability in many more advanced applications.[4-6] These porous crystals have emerged as potential candidates in a number of practical applications such as storage, separations, catalysis, sensing and supercapacitors are most of important usage of these porous materials.[3, 7] Through their high selectivity, quick-response, operability, and reversibility properties, MOFs be a great materials for sensing.[8] In addition, the size selectivity and the large surface areas of metal-organic frameworks can potentially be a reason for high sensibility detection of analytes.[9] Due to the advantages mentioned above, the use of a functionalized metal organic frameworks as a luminescent probe can be a good strategy to overcome some disadvantage of homogenous probe.[10, 11] This reflects the growing tendency of application of MOFs for detection and sensing of a wide verity of pollutants such as biomolecules, heavy metal ions, aromatic compounds and nitro aromatic materials.[12-14] Nitro explosive compounds are very electron deficient organic materials, can get electrons from electron donating compounds. Hence, the MOFs which have electron-donating group are great candidates for detecting nitro aromatic explosives such as 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (TNP).[15, 16] Amide group (-CO-NH-) is an interesting building block for designing new probes since i) carbonyl group $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{=}\mathbf{O})$ ) of amide can operate as acid while NH group consider as a base ii) amide itself can simultaneously donate hydrogen bonding (through –NH) as a one covalent acceptor from negative atoms from oxoanions like nitrate groups of nitro explosive or accept hydrogen bonding through the (–CO–) site iii) strong electrical field of carbonyl group and high polarizing ability for dipole-dipole/quadrupole interactions.[17, 18] Hence, introducing this highly efficient moiety into the porous structure of metal organic frameworks is the new strategy to synthesis new class of effective receptors.
The size and morphology of these functionalized frameworks play a crucial role in many applications.[19, 20] In order to achieve uniform structures, structure-directing agents or modulators such as HCl, benzoic acid, triethyl-amine, pyridine are introduced in the synthesis system. Space-filling modulators can be considered as templates to control the crystal growth direction which will regulate size and shape of the crystals and consequently uniformed MOF particles.[21, 22] Due to the great fundamental and technological interest and potential of nano-scale MOFs in a various application, in recent years, growing investigations have been focused on the design and synthesis of nano-scale frameworks.[23] Ultrasonic synthesis method has been widely used to construct nanoparticles with specific morphologies in recent years due to its rapid, economic and environmental friendly nature.[24] Using simultaneously ultrasonic irradiation along with different capping agent that can cap different facets of growing networks led to prepare nano-crystals with well-ordered morphologies.[25] This approach allows different functional groups to adhere selectively on to pore of crystalline structure which has direct effect in various application.[26, 27]
In this study, we first tried to synthesize MOFs with favorable functions by using solvothermal and ultrasonic methods (for nano scale) without any modulator and show fluorescence properties. We used the amide-group to decorate MOFs wall and combined it by different synthesis approaches to achieve effective luminescent probe for detection of nitroaromatic compounds. The fluorescence ability of Nano TMUs has compared with their balk crystals that are prepared by the conventional solvothermal method to understand the effect of enhancement of surface to volume ration on this process. Herein, inspired by this approach we report the synthesis of three new Nano luminescent MOFs containing acylamide group. Synthesized compounds are $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{oba})_{2}(\mathrm{bpfb})]{\cdot}(\mathrm{DMF})_{5}$ (Nano TMU-46), $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}(o b a)_{2}(b p t a)]\cdot(D M F)_{3}}}$ (Nano TMU-47); that the acylamide group in this compound is reverse in comparison to Nano TMU46; and $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{oba})_{2}(\mathrm{bpfn})]{\cdot}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}$ (Nano TMU-48), in which the use of the naphthalene analogue also effect on pore size and enforce the acylamide groups are more orientated into the cavities towards Nano TMU-46 and 47. In addition, we used the simple coordination modulation approach for the rational synthesis of nanorods and plate of $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{oba})_{2}(\mathrm{bpfb})]{\cdot}(\mathrm{DMF})_{5}$ (Nano TMU-46) to demonstrate the effect of morphology variation on luminescence behavior towards nirtoaromatics. The luminescence of Nanorods TMU-46 exhibit high selective sensitivity and quick response time in the presence of TNP in solution. Decoration of framework wall with accessible functional groups lead to stronger interaction and on the other hand using appropriate morphology cause fast diffusion and quick response.
