## Results and Discussion
## Crystal structure explanation
Our previous study demonstrate that the hydrothermal reaction between amide based N donor ligands including bpfb, bpta and bpfn (see Scheme.1) have led to successfully synthesis of three 3D amide-based metal organic frameworks.[28] The produced materials were labeled, TMU-46, TMU-47 and TMU-48. The structure of TMU-46 was characterized by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray crystallography but two other isoreticular frameworks were determined by using XRD, NMR, FTIR, TGA and BET. The X-ray analysis shows that TMU-46 crystallizes in monoclinic system with $\mathrm{~P~}2_{1}/\mathfrak{n}$ space group. TMU-46 and in accordance other amide based TMUs have a paddle-wheel structure, which is manufactured from a binuclear Cu unit $\mathrm{\DeltaCul}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2$ ). The $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}(C O O)_{4}}$ clusters pillared by amid $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ donor spacer ligands. (Fig.2)
The functional groups in ligands can easily undergo dimerization and oligomerization by formation of internal bond with each other (like hydrogen bond between N-H atoms of amide with O atoms of other amide groups) result in self-quenching of sorbent. One of the promising strategies to overcome this problem is to incorporate in MOFs. On the other hand using ultrasonic method for preparation of these functionalized MOFs could increase the surface-to-volume ratio which add considerable improvement in structures performance. In this investigation we prepared different morphologies of Nano-TMU-46, 47 and 48 by using ultrasonic irradiation in DMF. Fig 3, Shows the simulated XRD pattern from single crystal X-ray data of TMU-46 in comparison with the XRD patterns of samples of TMU-46, 47 and 48 prepared by the ultrasonic and solvothermal methods. This demonstrates that the compound which is synthesized by the sonochemical process as nanostructures is similar to that obtained by single crystal diffraction.
To study BET surface areas of the nanostructures TMUs have shown the ultrasonic assisted synthesis could increase surface area. The BET value for Nano TMU-46, 47 and 48 calculated to be 780, 790 and 820 $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. (Fig. 4).
## Optimization of ultrasonic method for synthesis of Nano TMU-46, 47, 48
In order to achieve different morphologies of nanomaterial, the effects of various parameters such as molar ratio of reactants, irradiation time and power, and capping agent been investigated. Two different concentration (0.01M and 0.05M) used for study the role of starting reactants on morphology and size of TMUs. Fig. 5 and Fig.6 displays the field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) of NanoTMU-46, 47 and 48 provided in different concentration of starting materials. It should be noted that the nano-cubic TMU-46, 47 and 48 obtained using lower concentration of $0.01\mathrm{{M}}$ (Fig. 5), while increasing the molar ratio of reactants up to 0.05M prepared the fine and agglomerate nano-plate morphology (Fig.
6). The results show that the size and morphology of the TMU-46, 47 and 48 nanostructure is also strongly depends on operating time and irradiation power. It can be clearly realized from the figure when the time is increased up to 2 hours for $0.01~\mathrm{M}$ , the  nano structure were agglomerated (Fig.7) and the adequate time to obtained best morphology is about 1 hour (Fig.5). Finally for investigation the effect of irradiation power on the nano structure shape and size, reactions were performed under various power ultrasound irradiation (12W, 18W and 24W). The obtained results of these sets of reactions are depicted in Figure 8. Using high irradiation power ( $18~\mathrm{w}$ , Fig. 8) leads to cubic-like morphology compared with lower powers (12W Fig. 8). Although, higher power ultrasound irradiation (24 W, Fig. 8) make nanostructures with non-uniform morphology.
## Effect of modulators on morphology of TMU-46
The synthesis process of crystalline material can be divided into nucleation and crystal growth. Any manipulation of these both steps enabled researchers rotationally control the size, morphology of nanoparticles. Using capping agent is more versatile as it offers the possibility to accomplish a more diverse morphology in MOFs. To investigate the effect of modulators on the size and morphology of TMU-46 particles, two modulators, benzoic acid and pyridine, were introduced into the synthesis process. Fig. 10 a, shows the BFDH (Bravais Friedel Donnay Harker) predicted morphology for TMU46 along the, a, b and c crystallography direction. As shown in Fig.10 d and f highly uniform rod-like particles were obtained by adding benzoic acid, however, plate-like particles were obtained by using pyridine as a modulator, and the size of particles dramatically increased. In fact, bases and acids can play different roles in directing the size and morphology of MOF particles, which will affect the growth process during the special direction.  Benzoic acid selectively impedes the Cu-oba coordination so nanorods are obtained solely. On the other hand pyridine compete with amide N donor linker because the lone pair on its nitrogen atom of pyridine is located in the same plane and direction with the conjugates with the $\pi$ electrons of the aromatic ring and the dipole moment. Also they will accelerate the crystal nucleation and growth process to assist the formation of MOF crystals. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the simulated and as-synthesized samples in the presence and absence of modulators are shown similar diffraction peaks.
