After examining the amide based nanostructures luminesces properties towards nitroaromatics, we have studied the effect of different morphology and size on fluorescence efficiency.  To examine the potential of both Rod and Plate TMU-46 the ethanol solution of TNP and TNT were gradually added into the dispersed MOFs and the PL properties are recorded. The luminescence intensity of Rod-TMU-46 decreases significantly when trivial concentration of TNT and TNP are added, but for Plate TMU-46 the luminescence intensity decreases slightly. The values of the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{SV}}$ quenching constant for Rod TMU-46 was found to be $16866\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ which is much higher for the fluorescence quenching of the Plate TMU-46 $(3019\ \mathrm{M}^{-1})$ ) in ethanol solution of TNP. For the observed contrasting difference there may be two plausible reasons (i) effect of size decreasing and expansion of surface area of fluorophore in rod morphology and (ii) increasing of amide group as main group for florescence quenching by adding capping benzoic acid agent. (Fig. 12)
## Conclusion
Three amide-based MOFs TMU-46, 47 and 48 have been successfully synthesized under ultrasound reaction and the florescence properties of these nano size structures have been investigated. We find out the effect of time, power irradiation of ultrasound concentrations and capping agent on size and morphology. Using different capping materials causes two different morphologies (Nanorod and Plate). The nano sized TMU-46 can selectively detect TNP with a very high rate of response in presence of other potentially interfering nitroaromatic explosives. More interestingly nanorod structure of TMU-46 which was achieved by using benzoic acid as a capping agent shows the significant improvement in detection of TNP. The detection limit of nanorod TMU-46 for the sensing of TNP was estimated to be $10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ , which is comparable with other MOF-based fluorescence sensors reported till date.
